{"artifact_id":"Figure3","kind":"figure","asset_path":"figures/fig_2506.07853v1-Figure3-1-e0b6842e0635.png","asset_sha256":"e0b6842e06354e26ded1508161d2c1528537078ddcf217fb2fa625742941f078","source_pdf_sha256":"bd601ac01957bfcb5ccaae523ecf11d324d21e2a6bb59ea8a094f1e6a1a8e906","revision_vid":"ecd9b6cd9f101cbe205827de598835d2aeb100f6184931ae36a8e2304f97b529","canonical_id":"2506.07853","section_vid":"8238e64fc1a6b2f7ad3b75dc9811b88158364ef11d0267e63ba08b312af2d66a","detector_fingerprint":"pdffigures2-0.0.12-SNAPSHOT","detector_metadata_sha256":"7a366ebfefd5e0db418c76730c263107d43c6df43be2a8755518a6e3e5020532","page_index":9,"region":{"x1":117.6,"y1":135.84,"x2":494.4,"y2":314.4},"caption":"Figure 3: Relationships among Temporal Versions reflecting the compilation process.","containing_section":{"relation":"containing","section_id":"sec-1~6","path_id":"1~6","title":"Figure 3 :","text":"3Figure 3: Relationships among Temporal Versions reflecting the compilation process.","truncated":false,"section_ordinal":19},"neighboring_sections":[{"relation":"previous","section_id":"sec-1~5","path_id":"1~5","title":"Figure 2 :","text":"2Figure 2: Monolingual case (Portuguese): Temporal Versions (TV) and Language Versions (LV) coincide.","truncated":false,"section_ordinal":18},{"relation":"next","section_id":"sec-1~7","path_id":"1~7","title":"Figure 3","text":"3Figure3illustrates this process: TV n is linked to TV n-1 and to all TV 0 of amending acts via the consolidates property, enabling deterministic reconstruction of the compiled (unofficial consolidated) text on any target date.By formalizing Compilation in this way, we ensure that every compiled (unofficial consolidated) Temporal Version can be precisely traced back to its immediate predecessor and all relevant amending acts, thereby supporting accurate, point-in-time retrieval and editorial consolidation of legal norms.\n\t\t\tLexML Brazil, Legislative Integration Project. Available at: https://projeto.lexml.gov.br. Accessed on: 08 Jun\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\tIt is important to clarify that this model represents the product of the legislative process-the final, enacted norm and its subsequent temporal states-rather than modeling the legislative procedure itself (e.g., bill drafting, debates, or voting)\n\t\t\tIn FRBRoo, an F15 ComplexWork represents a bibliographic Work that is composed of multiple intellectual Works, such as compilations or consolidated collections of laws.\n\t\t\tIn FRBRoo, an F4 ManifestationSingleton represents a unique physical object that embodies a single Expression, such as an original manuscript or signed decree.\n\t\t\tIn FRBRoo, an F22 SelfContainedExpression represents a complete, distinct realization of a Work's content.\n\t\t\tLexML URN syntax follows the standard proposed by the Brazilian LexML project. Available at: https://projeto.le xml.gov.br. Accessed on: 15 Mar 2025.\n\t\t\tLex","truncated":true,"section_ordinal":20}],"reference_excerpts":[{"section_id":"sec-1~6","path_id":"1~6","title":"Figure 3 :","matched_form":"figure 3","excerpt":"3Figure 3: Relationships among Temporal Versions reflecting the compilation process.","truncated":false}],"article_summary":{"source_id":"grobid:2506.07853#abstract","source_text_sha256":"cdaeefbc29177ef1f5519a4453f30b9929a891d2bdd5d6f6fa5becedba41a7ab","text":"Effectively representing legal norms for automated processing is a critical challenge, particularly in tracking the diachronic evolution of their hierarchical components (e.g., articles, paragraphs). While foundational frameworks like FRBR/FRBRoo and standards like Akoma Ntoso model legal documents at a macro level, they lack native mechanisms for granular, component-level versioning. This limitation hinders the deterministic point-in-time reconstruction of legal texts, a fundamental capability for reliable Legal Tech and AI applications. This paper proposes a structured, temporal model that extends the FRBRoo framework to address this gap. It introduces specialized subclasses of Expression-Temporal Version (TV) and Language Version (LV)-to represent the state of a legal norm and its linguistic variations at specific points in time. The model applies this same paradigm hierarchically, introducing Component Work (CW), Component Temporal Version (CTV), and Component Language Version (CLV) to track the lifecycle of individual articles, paragraphs, and clauses. Using the Brazilian Federal Constitution as a case study, the paper demonstrates how each amendment creates new Component Temporal Versions for affected provisions, while unaffected components retain their existing versions. This fine-grained, time-aware architecture enables the precise, deterministic retrieval and reconstruction of any part of a legal text as it existed on a specific date. The model provides a robust foundation for developing advanced legal information systems, knowledge graphs, and AI tools capable of accurate historical analysis and impact assessment, overcoming the limitations of current generative models.","truncated":false},"diagnostics":["neighbor_section_truncated:1~7"],"total_context_chars":3560,"truncated":true}